Our contribution presents an asynchronous decomposition algorithm for solving block-structured nonconvex optimization problems. This problem can be formulated as the following:

\begin{align*}
\min_{x_i \in \mathbb{R}^{n_i}} f_i (x_i) \, (i = 1, \ldots, m) \, \text{subject to} \, x = (x_1, \ldots, x_m) \in \prod_{i=1}^{m} \prod_{j=1}^{n_i} \mathbb{B}_j.
\end{align*}

Here each $f_i$ is a nonconvex function, and the outer product decomposition allows for the effective processing of large data sets on multiple processing units. Our algorithm dynamically decomposes the block iterative process into multiple asynchronous parallel sub-tasks that guarantee convergence under broad conditions to a critical point, and this convergence is proven using holomorphic functional theory.

